If the key generation isn't unique, you can encounter collisions, which can lead to incorrect data retrieval or overwriting of existing data. Here are several strategies to ensure unique keys and prevent collisions:

### 1. Use Unique Identifiers
Generate keys using unique identifiers such as GUIDs (Globally Unique Identifiers) or UUIDs (Universally Unique Identifiers). These identifiers are designed to be unique across space and time.

```csharp
Guid key = Guid.NewGuid();
```

### 2. Incorporate Timestamps
Include timestamps in your key generation to ensure uniqueness, especially if the keys are generated within a short period.

```csharp
string key = $"{DateTime.UtcNow.Ticks}_{Guid.NewGuid()}";
```

### 3. Use Composite Keys
Combine multiple pieces of information to form a composite key. This can be particularly useful if you have multiple attributes that can uniquely identify an item.

```csharp
string key = $"{userId}_{documentType}_{timestamp}";
```

### 4. Incremental Counters
Use an incremental counter to ensure uniqueness. This can be combined with other identifiers to form a unique key.

```csharp
int counter = 0;
string key = $"{counter++}_{userId}";
```

### 5. Hash-Based Keys
Use cryptographic hashes to generate keys. This can be useful if you want to derive a fixed-length key from variable-length data.

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

string GenerateHashKey(string input)
{
    using (SHA256 sha256Hash = SHA256.Create())
    {
        byte[] bytes = sha256Hash.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input));
        StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();
        foreach (byte b in bytes)
        {
            builder.Append(b.ToString("x2"));
        }
        return builder.ToString();
    }
}

string key = GenerateHashKey($"{userId}_{documentType}");
```

### 6. Namespace Prefixes
Prefix keys with namespaces to avoid collisions across different parts of your application.

```csharp
string key = $"user:{userId}:{documentType}";
```

### 7. Use a Centralized Key Generator
Implement a centralized key generator service that ensures uniqueness across all parts of your application. This can be particularly useful in distributed systems.

```csharp
public class KeyGenerator
{
    private static int _counter = 0;

    public static string GenerateKey(string userId, string documentType)
    {
        return $"{Interlocked.Increment(ref _counter)}_{userId}_{documentType}";
    }
}

string key = KeyGenerator.GenerateKey(userId, documentType);
```

### 8. Validate Uniqueness
Before inserting a key into the cache, check if it already exists. If it does, generate a new key until you find a unique one.

```csharp
public bool IsKeyUnique(string key)
{
    return !_cache.Contains(key);
}

string GenerateUniqueKey(string userId, string documentType)
{
    string key = $"{userId}_{documentType}";
    while (!IsKeyUnique(key))
    {
        key = $"{userId}_{documentType}_{Guid.NewGuid()}";
    }
    return key;
}

string uniqueKey = GenerateUniqueKey(userId, documentType);
```

### Summary
To prevent collisions in your caching system, ensure that your key generation strategy produces unique keys. Use GUIDs, timestamps, composite keys, incremental counters, hash-based keys, namespace prefixes, centralized key generators, or validate uniqueness before insertion. By implementing one or more of these strategies, you can significantly reduce the likelihood of key collisions and ensure the integrity of your caching system.

